WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.760
NFL, lead your team all the way to the Super Bowl.

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:27.700
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:29.260
I'm Mike. I'm Sean.

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:30.660
And I'm Joe.

00:00:31.580 --> 00:00:34.120
Football is back already?

00:00:34.660 --> 00:00:36.500
Well, not really.

00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:38.380
We're in the off-season in real life.

00:00:38.540 --> 00:00:40.580
But let's check in on those bills.

00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:42.120
Sean, how are those bills?

00:00:42.680 --> 00:00:50.160
Those bills actually exist for real this time and are not just a red and blue BUF logo.

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:58.040
Right. So this is another NES football game, but this time with the NFL license.

00:00:58.460 --> 00:00:59.320
I mean, think about it.

00:00:59.420 --> 00:01:03.560
They are bragging on the back of the box about being able to say Super Bowl.

00:01:03.560 --> 00:01:05.780
Nobody else is allowed to say Super Bowl.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:07.280
The NFL owns that.

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:10.680
They're letting you know that right away with the back of the box.

00:01:10.900 --> 00:01:15.320
However, you know, there's like no NFL PA.

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:17.860
So it's just NFL.

00:01:17.860 --> 00:01:20.620
There's no like players necessarily or any stats there.

00:01:20.620 --> 00:01:22.920
So it's not quite the complete package yet.

00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:29.540
But I could imagine a lot of people are the game is going to sell well because it has NFL on it.

00:01:29.540 --> 00:01:39.680
Yeah, this is I mean, the whole thing about the NFL PA might give you a better experience because, you know, that's your that's your team.

00:01:39.820 --> 00:01:40.720
Those are your guys.

00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:49.680
Whereas in this, like the NFL just sort of shows you like, OK, this is the product that the NFL thinks is good enough to sell me as the football simulation.

00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:51.240
So it's got to be good, right?

00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:55.820
Yeah, I hear this is what the players in the NFL actually use to train.

00:01:56.380 --> 00:02:00.520
Yeah. Actually, what's his name?

00:02:01.160 --> 00:02:06.280
Tom Bass is shouted out in the manual as I don't know.

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:09.140
He was probably an assistant coach in places.

00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:12.640
They said he has 20 years of NFL coaching experience.

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:18.420
And he was he was like a supervisor, not a supervisor, a consultant.

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:26.220
So that's their like appeal to authority to sell this game as a as the real football game.

00:02:26.840 --> 00:02:33.020
Yeah. I mean, they put it in bold on the box that this is the only licensed NFL NES game.

00:02:33.140 --> 00:02:40.340
So they wanted you to know when you go to the toy store and it's put right next to the other football games that this is the legal one.

00:02:40.520 --> 00:02:42.760
You're doing something wrong if you buy.

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:47.060
Strangely enough, though, only 10 yard fight and Tecmo Bowl, right?

00:02:47.060 --> 00:02:49.780
We haven't had any other football games to the best.

00:02:49.900 --> 00:02:51.060
Oh, that's it. No, that's not true.

00:02:51.220 --> 00:02:53.320
That that quarterback game.

00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:55.300
What was that guy?

00:02:56.140 --> 00:02:57.680
Remember? John Elway.

00:02:58.200 --> 00:02:59.420
Yeah. John Elway's quarterback.

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:02.260
So sorry. This is the fourth football game.

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:04.620
But John Elway also not the NFL.

00:03:04.840 --> 00:03:07.400
He was just a lone man acting alone.

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:08.660
That is just John Elway.

00:03:08.680 --> 00:03:09.700
He went rogue. Yeah.

00:03:13.320 --> 00:03:19.900
So it kind of has the same like layout as Tecmo Bowl.

00:03:20.060 --> 00:03:28.000
I mean, it kind of sucks because like this game comes out not too far removed from like a classic football game.

00:03:28.160 --> 00:03:34.040
Like the probably the best you're going to get from a football game in this era.

00:03:34.760 --> 00:03:36.340
And it looks kind of similar.

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:43.120
Yeah. And I mean, the appeal of an NFL game is having the NFL teams.

00:03:43.300 --> 00:03:47.080
But in this one, they didn't make the teams unique.

00:03:47.180 --> 00:03:50.620
You just choose the team that you want to play as.

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:53.240
So if you are a Bills fan, you would choose Buffalo.

00:03:53.440 --> 00:03:57.640
But then you're not greeted with like the stats and forget the players.

00:03:57.640 --> 00:04:01.300
You're not even greeted with the stats of the Bills for that season.

00:04:01.300 --> 00:04:07.040
And you are greeted with six potentially balanced types of teams that you choose from.

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:14.460
And on the one hand, this is great if your team sucked that year because now they don't suck in this version of the game.

00:04:14.560 --> 00:04:15.780
Like you don't really think about that.

00:04:15.880 --> 00:04:19.540
But there were many years as a Mets fan that I would buy baseball games and they were terrible.

00:04:19.720 --> 00:04:28.260
And it's like this isn't a fun experience unless you trade everybody that's on that team for, you know, you trade like three guys for one superstar or something.

00:04:28.260 --> 00:04:34.940
But Sean, since you are the de facto, you know, football fan here, I'm curious.

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:45.940
Did you think just as like a time capsule, does this help or hurt the game that you just basically the Bills and the Jets can theoretically be the same exact team?

00:04:46.260 --> 00:04:47.980
And they go up against each other.

00:04:48.200 --> 00:04:52.520
And the only difference now is one's on offense and one's on defense, depending on who has the ball.

00:04:52.520 --> 00:04:56.740
And this is like the last I'll say on the whole licensing thing.

00:04:57.600 --> 00:05:07.280
The only thing about this game that is affected by this is the fact that you are greeted by the NFL shield in the beginning of the game.

00:05:07.280 --> 00:05:12.920
You see the NFL shield in the middle of the field every time you play.

00:05:12.920 --> 00:05:21.160
Each of the end zones say NFL and you have the real team names in the select screen and on the scoreboard.

00:05:22.180 --> 00:05:23.840
It doesn't say anything about the city name.

00:05:24.040 --> 00:05:26.340
It doesn't have the the team logos.

00:05:26.620 --> 00:05:32.380
It doesn't have even the city name or the team logo on the field of which you play.

00:05:32.560 --> 00:05:41.740
It has a very like loose version of team colors for the players on the field.

00:05:42.120 --> 00:05:47.720
Really, the only thing that this NFL license did was put the team names in the game.

00:05:48.040 --> 00:05:54.960
That's it. Like each and you're right, like each team is just this.

00:05:54.960 --> 00:05:58.540
It's the same team that you can you can tweak if you want.

00:05:58.820 --> 00:05:59.660
Sorry. Go ahead, Joe.

00:05:59.960 --> 00:06:02.740
No, I was just going to say my, you know, my initial thought was like that.

00:06:03.020 --> 00:06:04.660
Okay, that was probably kind of touched on it.

00:06:04.720 --> 00:06:08.960
That was probably enough for them to just put the names in there.

00:06:09.080 --> 00:06:10.700
People are going to say, oh, my God, the NFL.

00:06:10.700 --> 00:06:15.440
I want it like they're like, we don't even need to go the extra mile and put like team stats or anything like that.

00:06:15.780 --> 00:06:18.300
But then I realized that they did put team stats.

00:06:18.440 --> 00:06:30.100
They just instead of having them apply to a particular team, they just let you select any of those stat layouts, which I mean, I guess still brings back to the question for you, Sean, if you thought it was better having it that way.

00:06:30.100 --> 00:06:39.660
But, you know, I thought like, well, if they if they went through the trouble of putting I mean, I know it was only six, but putting these six different types of team stats in the game.

00:06:39.660 --> 00:06:46.120
Why didn't they just assign those to different teams to give the teams some sort of reason for being separate teams?

00:06:46.120 --> 00:06:51.220
Even in the college football games and there is no college.

00:06:51.440 --> 00:06:57.880
There's no NCAA Players Association to like sign off on likenesses and name rights.

00:06:58.740 --> 00:07:11.680
Even then, like QB one for USC would still be like back in the day, like that that's still like close enough to Matt Leinert or something.

00:07:11.680 --> 00:07:18.260
Like it would be like this is what we think his stats would be, but we can't give him a name.

00:07:18.380 --> 00:07:21.620
But you can name a Matt Leinert if you want in the settings.

00:07:21.900 --> 00:07:29.940
And I would have expected like QB 12 on the Buffalo Bills to kind of have the same stats as Jim Kelly.

00:07:30.060 --> 00:07:31.040
But there is no number.

00:07:31.240 --> 00:07:37.580
There's just a character that is sort of lined up where a quarterback would be and he would throw the ball.

00:07:38.600 --> 00:07:44.220
They do include a handicap mode, which at first I avoided because I didn't really understand.

00:07:44.480 --> 00:07:50.780
You know, I thought that was like to give a crutch if you're playing with one player and another to be like, all right, I'll downgrade myself.

00:07:50.980 --> 00:07:53.840
Or, you know, you start the game with like extra points or something.

00:07:54.020 --> 00:07:54.840
It's not that.

00:07:55.000 --> 00:08:04.820
Instead, it's a way to customize the six team types so that you can change a C all the way up to an A or two Cs to a B.

00:08:05.120 --> 00:08:06.700
It's pretty customizable in that regard.

00:08:06.700 --> 00:08:09.580
I thought that was like another neat feature.

00:08:09.900 --> 00:08:29.160
But again, it seems like something where at that point, rather than just give the handicap to like specific point allotments or whatever, they should have just let you get like, you know, I'm thinking of Fallout or other games where you just basically start with all Fs and then you're given a certain amount of points where you build out your team.

00:08:29.160 --> 00:08:30.280
Point allocation, yeah.

00:08:30.520 --> 00:08:31.620
Exactly, exactly.

00:08:31.880 --> 00:08:35.840
You know, I think that would have been better than this handicap system, which is still nice to have.

00:08:35.960 --> 00:08:41.020
Don't get me wrong. And if this is, is it bases loaded that has like sort of, you can create a team?

00:08:41.240 --> 00:08:45.260
Which baseball game was that we played recently?

00:08:45.740 --> 00:08:47.300
And like you could play a whole season.

00:08:47.420 --> 00:08:48.220
Was that bases loaded?

00:08:48.500 --> 00:08:50.840
I thought it was baseball stars.

00:08:51.180 --> 00:08:52.060
Baseball stars. Yes.

00:08:52.060 --> 00:09:08.120
Okay. If I would have expected something like this to be in like an NFL analog to baseball stars where you're creating a football team and you're saying that, okay, this, this game, this, this quarterback's kind of, kind of shitty, but the offensive line and the running back are good.

00:09:08.120 --> 00:09:10.080
So that's, we're going to be a run first team.

00:09:10.220 --> 00:09:14.420
Like I would have expected this to be in a game like that, not in a game that has the NFL license.

00:09:14.420 --> 00:09:17.460
So it should reflect the NFL in some way.

00:09:18.520 --> 00:09:24.860
That's, that's the last that I'll, I'll harp on the kind of meaninglessness of this license.

00:09:24.860 --> 00:09:37.180
Well, I, I have one more thing to harp on with the teams and it's actually a funny thing that you mentioned earlier that I never thought about until you brought it up, but you were saying that it's weird that they don't have, or not that it's weird, but just that they don't mention the location.

00:09:37.180 --> 00:09:38.420
So you are the bills.

00:09:38.440 --> 00:09:40.100
You're not the Buffalo bills.

00:09:40.280 --> 00:09:42.820
You sure are, but it doesn't say it anywhere.

00:09:43.140 --> 00:09:53.240
Um, it doesn't even like list the city or anything that actually works in the favor of the Rams and the Raiders, you know, like now, now those teams take place in different cities and it doesn't feel dated.

00:09:53.240 --> 00:10:00.360
I don't know though, if you guys are like me, sports games on any console were always like cheaper if they were older.

00:10:00.360 --> 00:10:07.600
And it was fun to like go back and play that year's stats of things with that year's players or whatever.

00:10:07.840 --> 00:10:13.180
And just like, for me, that was another way because keep in mind, we didn't have YouTube and all that.

00:10:13.360 --> 00:10:19.940
It was another way to like learn about what happened or who was good those years in, uh, in those particular sports.

00:10:20.220 --> 00:10:22.300
And so that element is lost here.

00:10:22.580 --> 00:10:25.880
Absolutely. What did you guys think of the football itself?

00:10:26.960 --> 00:10:28.380
Oh, I forgot about the football.

00:10:28.740 --> 00:10:34.400
So you bring up a good point, Sean, because we, I feel like let's not break down football again for everyone.

00:10:34.800 --> 00:10:39.360
Um, I'm sure by now any sport it's, it seems silly to do that.

00:10:39.360 --> 00:10:48.660
So what I wanted to talk about is the flow of the game, because that is the thing that changes from, um, developer to developer is yeah.

00:10:48.660 --> 00:10:53.000
They're going to follow the rules of, of the sport as closely as they choose to.

00:10:53.000 --> 00:10:57.260
And they're going to, you know, try to make it look like a game of football.

00:10:57.260 --> 00:11:02.700
But the flow of NFL is arguably much different than tech mobile.

00:11:02.700 --> 00:11:13.540
And I think the biggest thing to notice is that they're, you know, as a, as a low informed watcher, just somebody who watches football casually doesn't really understand the strategy of anything.

00:11:14.240 --> 00:11:19.740
It's mostly a game that is very segmented between plays and play book.

00:11:19.740 --> 00:11:25.160
You know, that time where like they're coming up with the plays and the, the clock's counting down, but there's no actual action going on.

00:11:25.260 --> 00:11:31.560
And then the actual play of the down, which is, you know, something that happens very quickly in this game.

00:11:32.100 --> 00:11:38.360
Both sections of that are, are almost like exaggeratedly long for a video game.

00:11:38.480 --> 00:11:44.580
Oh yeah. Both figuring out which play you want to do something you have to refer to in a separate sheet.

00:11:44.580 --> 00:11:50.660
And then also the experience of being within that down, uh, I don't know what we call those things, Sean.

00:11:50.820 --> 00:11:51.780
Uh, is it a play?

00:11:52.540 --> 00:11:55.580
Um, each, I mean, each down, I guess is, okay.

00:11:55.780 --> 00:11:59.660
Cool. Yeah. So yeah, each play, like how long that takes to play out.

00:11:59.780 --> 00:12:02.120
I don't know. The flow felt very interrupted here.

00:12:02.700 --> 00:12:10.560
Go for it. Uh, I I'd say that, um, when, when you play tech mobile and I'm always going to compare it to that.

00:12:10.900 --> 00:12:12.360
Um, it's very streamlined.

00:12:12.360 --> 00:12:19.920
You get, you get like a play select and you pick your play and then you're immediately brought to the field to execute your play.

00:12:20.500 --> 00:12:31.240
And then in, uh, in this game, you, you, you're looking at your scoreboard and then you sort of do this blind play select.

00:12:31.240 --> 00:12:39.060
Even if you're playing against the computer that you have to like, like Mike said, refer to your manual to know what play you're going to pick.

00:12:39.060 --> 00:12:46.100
And then it's going to show you like, um, like the, the, the defensive lineup.

00:12:46.360 --> 00:12:49.040
Like you're going to see how many defensive backs they got.

00:12:49.040 --> 00:12:56.200
And they're going to show like your, uh, your, your, uh, receivers and all that shit.

00:12:56.380 --> 00:13:05.140
And then you're going to see your guys and then you're going to hike the ball and run or pass.

00:13:05.140 --> 00:13:16.180
It's, it's, it's even, there's even more steps like where, like exactly like football is a very start and stop sport, um, where everything happens all at once.

00:13:16.440 --> 00:13:17.340
And then nothing happens.

00:13:17.480 --> 00:13:21.940
Uh, this just sort of adds even more not happening to the game.

00:13:21.940 --> 00:13:24.280
And I never really, I don't, I don't really get it.

00:13:24.400 --> 00:13:29.580
Like a lot of this can be one screen, uh, but they choose to split it up between five.

00:13:30.460 --> 00:13:44.540
Yeah. I think that, you know, my experience maybe wasn't quite as bad because I will confess that I almost never referred to, referred to the like plays for like what the inputs are the plays after like a couple of minutes of playing this game.

00:13:44.640 --> 00:13:47.100
I was like, you know what? I'm just going to pick random plays and hope that they work.

00:13:47.180 --> 00:13:50.140
Cause I do not feel like looking up these things every two seconds.

00:13:50.140 --> 00:13:52.500
Cause that just takes, it takes forever.

00:13:52.700 --> 00:13:56.140
I'm granted like, I don't know what a good alternative is.

00:13:56.240 --> 00:14:03.180
If you really want to get into like having all these different plays and stuff, like I can see this being a positive, but for me, I was just like, I just kind of want to see how the play, how the game plays.

00:14:03.520 --> 00:14:06.820
So yeah, I ended up kind of abandoning that pretty quickly.

00:14:07.440 --> 00:14:15.700
The one thing that I did like, and like any play can pretty much be a run or a pass play, um, which is fine.

00:14:15.700 --> 00:14:25.720
Um, but what I did like is when you do end up passing and there are several steps to actually throwing the ball, which I don't really want to get into, but there's more steps than there should be.

00:14:25.720 --> 00:14:39.160
Um, it will do, it'll zoom in to your receiver, uh, to, to sort of give you a bit more, uh, uh, uh, get more to work with in terms of, are you going to receive this ball?

00:14:39.260 --> 00:14:40.580
Is this ball going to get deflected?

00:14:40.620 --> 00:14:42.020
Is this ball going to be intercepted?

00:14:42.220 --> 00:14:48.920
Um, I liked that it's, it's a bit more like cinematic than, uh, like a Tecmo.

00:14:48.920 --> 00:14:55.260
Uh, but again, it's, it's all in the execution and it's, it's sort of falls flat.

00:14:55.440 --> 00:15:02.180
It's adding more stops to the play now within the play, uh, which this game does not need.

00:15:03.100 --> 00:15:12.820
Um, and then if you just zoom out to the game at large, like I have never seen it since 10 yard fight, I have not seen football players move this slowly.

00:15:12.820 --> 00:15:17.680
And it, it doesn't even like, it's not even just that the players aren't moving slowly.

00:15:17.720 --> 00:15:25.720
The, the, the screen is scrolling slowly and it looks like they're all doing this weird, like strut instead of running.

00:15:26.160 --> 00:15:27.700
They're like strutting down the field.

00:15:27.700 --> 00:15:32.680
And then the, the camera just jerks down the field in time with the strut.

00:15:32.840 --> 00:15:33.720
It's, it's comical.

00:15:33.880 --> 00:15:40.180
I really, I don't know how else to describe it other than movement in this game feeling comically like a dance.

00:15:40.680 --> 00:15:58.060
And it feels bad from the moment you have the ball or, you know, like what in your, when you're in that 40 yard screen, but there is a moment during passing plays where it slows down even further as an intentional slow-mo so that you can focus on like who's right.

00:15:58.240 --> 00:16:00.520
Who's open and what you want to do and stuff like that.

00:16:00.520 --> 00:16:03.760
I, I just found it, it was all happening.

00:16:03.940 --> 00:16:16.020
Like those plays were happening way too long and everything, but also the breakup between the three stages of the scoreboard, the playbook, the 40 yard screen, the 15 yard zoom in to the 40 yard screen.

00:16:16.020 --> 00:16:30.060
Like I can't even imagine how long an actual game of NFL would have taken me to play out, especially because I was trying to play this authentically with the offensive playbook, which is as far as I know, separate from the manual.

00:16:30.060 --> 00:16:34.760
It's its own separate instruction sheet, which kind of makes it like a neat relic.

00:16:34.900 --> 00:16:41.740
Obviously it was translated to game facts, but I just feel like it's, it's strange on one level.

00:16:41.740 --> 00:16:44.840
It's a unique way to create a lot of different plays, right?

00:16:45.340 --> 00:16:49.100
It takes away the ability to screen look at all.

00:16:49.320 --> 00:16:50.180
You lock it.

00:16:50.280 --> 00:16:57.000
It has an NES controller and you lock in your picks and the offense doesn't know what the defense is doing and so on and so forth.

00:16:57.500 --> 00:17:09.800
You have the ability to call out audibles in the, in the, before the balls hike too, which is like a really interesting extra mechanic, but it feels overly complicated for the product.

00:17:09.800 --> 00:17:19.000
I get that they were going for something more true to the NFL experience, being able to even have penalties like, um, face mask and stuff like that.

00:17:19.080 --> 00:17:21.020
Like these are all cool, neat touches.

00:17:21.020 --> 00:17:29.220
It just actually makes for a slower, boring experience when it actually like happens in practice.

00:17:29.220 --> 00:17:43.360
And especially because when you read the manual, um, it outright tells you that it, it, this gets modulated by if you use like handicaps or, uh, upgrade your stats at all.

00:17:43.360 --> 00:17:51.240
But the game outright says, and then two penalties per team will randomly be, uh, will be assorted.

00:17:51.460 --> 00:17:52.700
And I'm like, wait a second.

00:17:52.840 --> 00:17:53.700
Don't tell me that.

00:17:53.840 --> 00:18:06.000
Like that, that has always been a complaint of football games is that penalties like face mask and penalties like, uh, unsportsmanlike have always been random.

00:18:06.000 --> 00:18:16.560
And now you're just telling me that like, even without the added simulation of, of the more recent games, now it's just going to happen whenever.

00:18:17.480 --> 00:18:18.500
I don't know.

00:18:18.620 --> 00:18:47.080
That's funny. The manual even goes as far as to give you in the strategy section, just a, a talking to about like how you're not going to have fun at first.

00:18:47.260 --> 00:18:48.960
Like you don't, don't think about having fun.

00:18:49.160 --> 00:18:50.840
You have to learn how to play this game.

00:18:50.960 --> 00:18:57.040
The beginning player might be overwhelmed by all of the options and the complexity of the controls in this game.

00:18:57.180 --> 00:18:58.240
Don't be discouraged.

00:18:58.280 --> 00:19:02.260
In order to play like the pros, you have to put in some practice time.

00:19:02.820 --> 00:19:05.080
That's only paragraph one of them.

00:19:05.220 --> 00:19:15.020
What is a second paragraph of a very long detailed explanation about why you shouldn't even attempt passing until you figured out the running game and stuff like it's just like, it's nonsense.

00:19:15.020 --> 00:19:19.380
It's like telling people in Super Mario Brothers, like, don't, you're not ready for the fire flower.

00:19:19.700 --> 00:19:22.800
Like just learn how to jump over the pitfalls first, buddy.

00:19:23.280 --> 00:19:29.660
Yeah. But I mean, maybe they were, they realized like, oh, like this game is complicated for our young audience.

00:19:29.660 --> 00:19:33.700
Maybe we need to like get ahead of that right away before they just put the game down.

00:19:33.900 --> 00:19:42.360
And I could understand that if it was actually a challenging game, but the compute, you know, all the complexities they've put in, the computer can be very easily cheesed.

00:19:42.440 --> 00:19:56.980
I'm not saying the computer's bad by any means, or it's not a difficult game, but I found that just picking the running play within four downs, I was probably going to gain enough yards to get the first down and just continue to repeat that kind of action.

00:19:56.980 --> 00:20:05.240
In some ways, the computer is just a practice mode in this game for the actual human versus human element that would probably be a lot more exciting.

00:20:05.920 --> 00:20:19.020
Yeah. And I think just like, but we already complained about how like as a baseline presentation, there isn't much in terms of the differences between teams and how they use the license.

00:20:19.020 --> 00:20:31.540
But like even you don't even need to have anybody's face licensed, like have there be a little cut scene of, of a touchdown, like in Tecmo Bowl, like make it fun in some way.

00:20:31.660 --> 00:20:34.620
This because the simulation doesn't mean it doesn't have to be fun.

00:20:34.740 --> 00:20:40.360
No matter if Tom Bass wrote a strongly worded letter in the manual saying that you shouldn't have fun.

00:20:40.360 --> 00:20:42.460
Like you can still do stuff like that.

00:20:43.000 --> 00:20:50.120
Yeah. Especially because they did have, they already have in this game like that close up for when you're receiving a pass, they could just do something similar.

00:20:50.280 --> 00:20:51.760
Spike the ball. Yeah.

00:20:51.940 --> 00:20:54.580
They already had like animations like that.

00:20:57.100 --> 00:20:58.400
Can I back up a bit?

00:20:58.400 --> 00:21:10.660
I want to get your guys' like deeper thoughts on, on that animation when you're receiving the ball, because I had an interesting kind of process with that.

00:21:10.720 --> 00:21:12.580
Cause at first I was like, that's really annoying.

00:21:12.740 --> 00:21:19.700
And I did feel like that like interrupts the gameplay in a weird way that like, like, why am I going to a different view now?

00:21:19.780 --> 00:21:21.920
No, you know, no other football game has done it this way.

00:21:22.380 --> 00:21:25.900
But as I played it, I actually grew to really appreciate that part.

00:21:26.760 --> 00:21:34.160
It actually made it feel like receiving a pass was a little more, I had a little more agency in it.

00:21:34.240 --> 00:21:39.740
It was a little more like, rather than it just being like, oh yeah, it was generally in the right area.

00:21:39.740 --> 00:21:46.260
So I caught the pass where it's like, oh no, it now zooms in and there'll be like, you know, the receiver is there.

00:21:46.420 --> 00:21:48.940
And also like whoever is defending you is there.

00:21:49.100 --> 00:21:54.260
And if you're playing two player, it feels a little more satisfying to get an interception because you feel like you did it.

00:21:54.260 --> 00:22:02.560
You have to jump and you have to grab the ball, you know, and, and, and it felt, you know, as much as it is a little bit of like a weird interruption that made it more fun.

00:22:02.680 --> 00:22:05.380
And full disclosure, I only played this game two player.

00:22:05.760 --> 00:22:11.660
Um, and, and in that way it was fun, but I wonder if it was, uh, how the computer handled that.

00:22:12.140 --> 00:22:16.340
I mean, the computer, it, it, it, it can catch.

00:22:16.340 --> 00:22:19.680
And I had actually been intercepted.

00:22:19.700 --> 00:22:21.660
I've been intercepted in this game too.

00:22:21.860 --> 00:22:25.220
So it's not like it's a, it's a gimme, uh, every time.

00:22:25.280 --> 00:22:30.500
And I do like that you sort of will control your receiver after the fact.

00:22:31.120 --> 00:22:41.460
Um, I, I just think that the, the cons sort of outweigh the pros in terms of the, uh, because these games are so long,

00:22:41.460 --> 00:22:46.260
like you're actually going to play this game for an hour if you want to play the full game.

00:22:46.380 --> 00:22:50.340
Cause you don't, you can't play, uh, in a bridged game.

00:22:50.540 --> 00:22:52.700
It's like, I think they're all 15 minute quarters.

00:22:53.200 --> 00:22:56.600
Right. And it's a slow clock.

00:22:57.620 --> 00:23:04.200
So when you add in all of these little things, it just becomes a slog.

00:23:04.200 --> 00:23:08.280
And I do agree that at its basis level, it's a cool idea.

00:23:08.280 --> 00:23:15.520
I just think that it's execution, uh, ends up harming the pace more than it helps like the engagement.

00:23:16.140 --> 00:23:23.280
And even that 15 yard screen that we're the zoom in screen, it doesn't that feel like a little empty?

00:23:23.620 --> 00:23:29.140
Isn't it weird that there's only ever like three characters maximum in that particular part of the field?

00:23:29.260 --> 00:23:33.980
I don't know football that well, but like when I see them on TV, it looks like they're, you know,

00:23:33.980 --> 00:23:40.960
it's either a really deep pass where it's just two guys or even just looking at the 40 yard screen here.

00:23:41.440 --> 00:23:43.560
There's, it seems like there's a lot of action happening.

00:23:43.560 --> 00:23:49.080
And then they just microscopic down to just two players.

00:23:49.080 --> 00:23:53.280
You know, it's like, how zoomed in are we that we can't see the other guys on the field?

00:23:53.280 --> 00:24:10.300
I'll say that, um, just by virtue of the, the players in this game walking through molasses, um, you, you will very, you'll very rarely be able to get to a, like a one-on-one situation where it's just like a corner and a wide receiver.

00:24:10.300 --> 00:24:13.840
And that's all that's going to be in that 15 yard zoom in screen.

00:24:14.220 --> 00:24:28.700
Um, because most of the time you're not gonna be able to get off a pass, uh, or, or you'll be surrounded by defenders as the quarterback and your receivers will still just be like 10 yards down the field, um, surrounded by defenders as well.

00:24:28.700 --> 00:24:39.760
So, so yeah, I, I do think that it, uh, maybe they're cutting some defenders, uh, to, to make that, like that secondary screen more manageable.

00:24:39.760 --> 00:24:42.420
I don't actually know, but you're right.

00:24:42.800 --> 00:24:45.500
It, it, now that I think about it, it does feel a little off.

00:24:46.780 --> 00:24:55.120
Yeah. You know what? I forgot when I mentioned, uh, the positives about that, that I did also want to, uh, follow that up with one negative about that, uh, closeup screen.

00:24:55.120 --> 00:25:09.060
And that is that the, it appears to me and correct me if you guys felt that this was not true, but it appears to me that that closeup, you know, microscope view is not actually laid out.

00:25:09.180 --> 00:25:12.740
The players are not actually in the, in the same position that they are in the overall.

00:25:13.000 --> 00:25:14.080
That's sort of what I'm saying. Yeah.

00:25:14.420 --> 00:25:18.100
Okay. So when it, yeah, when it cuts back, I'm always like for a second, a little confused.

00:25:18.260 --> 00:25:20.220
Where am I? And, and who am I?

00:25:20.600 --> 00:25:21.920
Who's, who's defending me?

00:25:21.920 --> 00:25:27.800
Like, like it'll cut back and I will run right into like a tackle because I didn't know that guy was there.

00:25:27.900 --> 00:25:31.880
Cause he wasn't. He wasn't there when you were zoomed in, but now he's there.

00:25:32.020 --> 00:25:43.600
Yeah. It's like, uh, in, uh, I forgot what, endless legend, like where, when you meet on the hex grid, then you start another like mini battle within that hex.

00:25:43.600 --> 00:25:52.220
And then you come back out of it and like, it's, it's all just different, uh, abstractions of, of football instead of football.

00:25:52.780 --> 00:25:54.360
It's like a random encounter.

00:25:54.540 --> 00:25:55.620
Yeah, exactly. Yeah.

00:25:59.040 --> 00:26:13.800
A thing I could appreciate though, about the presentation of the game is how much information is conveyed to you in a simple, effective manner, both on the 40 yard screen, where you have your time on the clock, uh, your downs and how much you have to go.

00:26:13.800 --> 00:26:27.920
But also in the scoreboard, there's a lot of information there in terms of, you know, both the players selecting their inputs, uh, that they're going to make for their play selection, but also the nice like referee graphics that change depending on the situation.

00:26:28.280 --> 00:26:37.060
What, um, I feel like they didn't leave anything out, uh, that they could have, you know, like I know there's no, um, there's no challenging plays or anything like that.

00:26:37.060 --> 00:26:52.220
But, you know, for the most part, everything that you would expect to need to know as a coach of an NFL team in terms of the play selection and where you are in the game, it's all like laid out very nicely and almost, uh, like a TV experience.

00:26:52.560 --> 00:26:55.980
Yeah. And as far as I can tell, like they did model the game out correctly.

00:26:56.100 --> 00:27:07.040
Like I did a couple of tests, like a safety ends up being a safety, um, the, uh, like it will sort of default you to a punt and then you'll actually get like a punt return.

00:27:07.060 --> 00:27:19.660
Turner in the back, uh, when, when it is, uh, when, when you actually are punting, like they, they do model out the game correctly and you do have all the information that you need to play it within the constraints that they've given you.

00:27:20.120 --> 00:27:25.460
Um, and yeah, I do like some of the little graphics they show, but they could have done a lot more.

00:27:25.700 --> 00:27:27.140
Oh yeah. That's the personality.

00:27:27.440 --> 00:27:35.720
That's the only thing I like presentation wise, actually, because if you think about even just the field of the game, you were talking about how they have the NFL end zones.

00:27:35.720 --> 00:27:41.440
That would have been a perfect opportunity to get some team logos in there that are sorely missing in this game.

00:27:41.700 --> 00:27:49.640
But even in 15 minute quarters, the quarters that you must play through, there is no halftime show in the middle.

00:27:49.800 --> 00:27:54.520
Something that would have been very easy to do and could have been fun with some, uh, nice sprite work.

00:27:54.520 --> 00:28:03.200
There could have been, um, information before the game about just like made up stats or something that you get to see some kind of opening ceremony or something.

00:28:03.200 --> 00:28:06.960
There's plenty that we've seen in other sports games that I'm just riffing on here.

00:28:07.080 --> 00:28:08.360
I'm not coming up with anything original.

00:28:08.400 --> 00:28:20.800
It just feels strange that the game is so straightforward that it wants you to just pretty much have like the coaching experience of, of a football game and not much else.

00:28:21.260 --> 00:28:23.080
Yeah. I agree.

00:28:23.380 --> 00:28:25.060
I agree as well.

00:28:25.060 --> 00:28:28.040
Although I'll say I don't miss the halftime show type thing.

00:28:28.200 --> 00:28:30.180
Usually that's like funny to watch once.

00:28:30.360 --> 00:28:33.380
And then I'm kind of like, okay, let me just play this game.

00:28:34.260 --> 00:28:39.920
That's fair. You don't like the little ETs in soccer?

00:28:40.680 --> 00:28:42.060
Oh, I forgot about that.

00:28:45.560 --> 00:28:57.640
So this game was the first NFL game since NFL football, which was released all the way back in 1979, 10 years prior for the Intellivision.

00:28:58.080 --> 00:29:07.020
And I took a look at that game and let's just say, you know, it is actually a nice improvement over, you know, like the NES did.

00:29:07.340 --> 00:29:12.860
When you think about everything that came before it, like we're talking about NES games in 2022 and occasionally we'll complain about the graphics.

00:29:13.020 --> 00:29:14.800
But I think we have a high tolerance.

00:29:15.160 --> 00:29:19.160
When I look at, when I look at in television games, they're unplayable.

00:29:20.440 --> 00:29:21.980
Yeah. They're just like lines, right?

00:29:22.040 --> 00:29:26.020
It's like a line with like a, with like a, with a suggestion of a head on top of it.

00:29:26.660 --> 00:29:28.920
That's more Atari 2600, but yeah.

00:29:29.500 --> 00:29:31.860
No, Intellivision gets that way too, for the most part.

00:29:32.060 --> 00:29:43.140
So I think it's just, in a way, this is a good experience for people who, you know, at this day and age, like who just wanted the NFL like team of their choice.

00:29:43.260 --> 00:29:52.340
Right. Theoretically, Sean, if you were buying NES games in 1989, you would have likely have bought this one just to be able to play as the Bills.

00:29:52.480 --> 00:29:53.340
Right. Sure.

00:29:53.600 --> 00:29:54.940
That would have meant something to you.

00:29:54.940 --> 00:30:00.560
I definitely would have already owned Tecmo Bowl and I would have just continued to play Tecmo Bowl.

00:30:01.520 --> 00:30:02.560
Got it. Okay.

00:30:02.780 --> 00:30:04.680
Fair enough. Oh, shit.

00:30:04.720 --> 00:30:06.500
Well. This isn't the essential games list.

00:30:06.700 --> 00:30:21.440
Right, right, right. You're going to have to probably say that again because we are about to do the one thing we must do every episode and that is decide whether NFL is an essential game on the essential games list.

00:30:21.440 --> 00:30:23.920
An essential organization. It just, yeah, it felt weird, right?

00:30:24.020 --> 00:30:27.820
It's like, are we, the game is just called NFL or is it called NFL football?

00:30:28.120 --> 00:30:29.940
On the box, it's just NFL.

00:30:29.940 --> 00:30:35.040
Well, so if it is called NFL football, tell me where because I don't see that anywhere.

00:30:35.140 --> 00:30:40.060
But also, that would be strange because that would mean that it would be called National Football League football.

00:30:43.260 --> 00:30:45.920
Sean, your vote once more.

00:30:45.920 --> 00:30:49.300
Yeah, I already said what I said.

00:30:50.600 --> 00:30:51.760
Love football.

00:30:52.180 --> 00:30:59.000
I mean, any chance I get to play it in some digital way, I will.

00:31:00.200 --> 00:31:05.620
But, I mean, we've already come a long way with, I'll say it again, Tecmo Bowl.

00:31:06.360 --> 00:31:08.060
It is an essential game.

00:31:08.780 --> 00:31:12.800
And this NFL, NFL game is not.

00:31:14.520 --> 00:31:15.480
Back to you.

00:31:16.020 --> 00:31:17.300
Back to you, Joe.

00:31:17.620 --> 00:31:21.460
What do you think? I was going to just go, but he threw it back to you.

00:31:21.520 --> 00:31:23.620
So, I had to wait for you to call on me.

00:31:26.280 --> 00:31:36.080
You know, like I said, I played this two-player and I always think that, well, I usually think that that is a recipe for having a little more fun than playing single-player anyways.

00:31:37.060 --> 00:31:41.200
And in that sense, we didn't not have fun.

00:31:41.400 --> 00:31:46.060
But still, even then, it was like, God, this game is so long.

00:31:46.460 --> 00:31:47.920
And it is a little tedious.

00:31:48.280 --> 00:31:49.200
It took a while.

00:31:49.200 --> 00:31:55.560
And I'll say that part of the fun was just like figuring out how the game worked on the fly.

00:31:55.800 --> 00:32:00.480
And I think like once we both figured out how the game worked, like nobody scored again.

00:32:00.940 --> 00:32:02.080
I was going to ask.

00:32:02.440 --> 00:32:03.920
I don't know what that means.

00:32:04.900 --> 00:32:07.280
Like if the game is too easy or too hard.

00:32:07.680 --> 00:32:09.880
But like. I was going to ask who won.

00:32:10.840 --> 00:32:13.320
He won. My buddy Eric won.

00:32:13.420 --> 00:32:14.740
It was not friend of the show, Mark.

00:32:15.540 --> 00:32:17.960
But it was friend of the show, new friend of the show, Eric.

00:32:17.960 --> 00:32:19.240
Eric, old friend of mine.

00:32:19.400 --> 00:32:21.180
I don't know why I'm going on so much about this.

00:32:21.340 --> 00:32:22.320
This game is not essential.

00:32:22.600 --> 00:32:24.800
But it was like sort of fun to play two-player.

00:32:25.100 --> 00:32:27.980
You know, I'm not a football game guy.

00:32:28.220 --> 00:32:30.400
So, you know, take that for what you will.

00:32:30.620 --> 00:32:38.000
I just noticed that the only place where you can find any of the team logos on this product is in the manual.

00:32:38.180 --> 00:32:40.400
It's the very first thing they want you to know.

00:32:41.200 --> 00:32:43.740
Who's in the AFC versus who's in the NFC.

00:32:43.940 --> 00:32:47.380
Before they even tell you to treat your interactive game carefully.

00:32:47.700 --> 00:32:50.980
Or give you an introduction on the lowdown of what it means to play this game.

00:32:51.160 --> 00:32:53.980
They give you those logos and then you never see them again.

00:32:54.460 --> 00:32:55.760
A weird touch again.

00:32:55.900 --> 00:32:57.900
Just like a lot of weird touches in this game.

00:32:58.760 --> 00:33:01.280
We didn't mention that this is an LJN game.

00:33:01.600 --> 00:33:02.860
Not that it really matters.

00:33:02.860 --> 00:33:08.400
But it does seem like once again LJN has found a way to just convince another person.

00:33:08.400 --> 00:33:10.520
To let them like license their products.

00:33:10.520 --> 00:33:13.660
That they could make some half-baked game.

00:33:14.020 --> 00:33:16.680
Because yeah, we do have Tecmo Bowl.

00:33:16.780 --> 00:33:19.380
But I would argue forget about what we have.

00:33:19.540 --> 00:33:25.360
And just think about like if this was the only football game that we had access to on the NES.

00:33:25.600 --> 00:33:26.520
I don't.

00:33:26.660 --> 00:33:28.540
I think I wouldn't be satisfied at all.

00:33:28.660 --> 00:33:31.540
Like I think I would still have these same complaints about the flow of the game.

00:33:31.540 --> 00:33:33.320
And how long it takes.

00:33:33.880 --> 00:33:38.860
It's nice that nine innings of baseball on any of the seven baseball games we've played.

00:33:39.120 --> 00:33:43.180
Hasn't felt like it would take me an hour plus to get through them.

00:33:43.340 --> 00:33:47.960
And yet this game, I never completed a full game just because I could feel the slog.

00:33:48.600 --> 00:33:52.520
I was happy to play test other teams and see like if there was balance changes.

00:33:52.700 --> 00:33:55.240
But the actual like experience of playing a full game.

00:33:55.340 --> 00:33:58.020
I don't know. It never appealed to me.

00:33:58.160 --> 00:34:00.180
And so that's like a warning sign right there.

00:34:00.180 --> 00:34:03.860
If you can't even get through a full simulation of a sports game.

00:34:03.940 --> 00:34:06.400
Let alone you're probably supposed to play 16 of these right.

00:34:06.540 --> 00:34:09.720
Or 17. Or how many football games they play these days of the season.

00:34:09.820 --> 00:34:10.960
Now it's 17.

00:34:11.180 --> 00:34:12.880
But there's no season mode in this.

00:34:13.020 --> 00:34:14.560
Right. There's no season mode in this either.

00:34:14.740 --> 00:34:17.140
So that's why it's not on the essential game.

